 ### Edge cases
 
-- **Heredoc bodies above the monitor.** The 3-non-blank-lines window for the `monitor_rc=` init check must skip heredoc body content so `cat <<'EOF' ... monitor_rc=0 ... EOF` does not satisfy the check. Reuse the `try_begin_heredoc` / `heredoc_close_matches` heredoc-tracking already present in `scan_fence_buffer_for_anchors` (lines 545-607, 644-654). Apply the same tracking inside the backward walk so heredoc bodies count as opaque and do not consume the 3-line window. Conservative behavior: treat the entire heredoc as a single "non-counting" region; do not advance the non-blank counter inside heredoc bodies.
-- **Backslash-continuation on the monitor.** Check (2) operates on the merged logical line, so multi-line monitor invocations (the canonical shape in `BASH_AUTHORING.md` §4) match correctly. Use the same merging helper as the existing `&` check.
-- **Comment-only lines between writer and monitor.** A `# foo` line is not blank for shell purposes but is conceptually skippable. The init walk skips both blank and comment-only lines so a fence with `monitor_rc=0  # init` followed by `# comment\n# comment\n# comment\nmonitor.sh` still satisfies the check.
-- **Conditional inside heredoc after monitor.** Check (3) must also skip heredoc bodies in the forward scan so a heredoc body containing the literal word `if monitor_rc` does not falsely satisfy the check. Reuse the same heredoc tracking.
-- **`monitor_rc` reference in conditional body but keyword on prior line.** A shell like `if true; then\n  case "$monitor_rc" in ... esac\nfi` should satisfy. Match-on-keyword-line-or-any-subsequent-line-through-end-of-fence keeps the implementation simple. False positives where `monitor_rc` appears in an unrelated late conditional are acceptable — they signal that the operator did intend to use `monitor_rc`, just not in the canonical shape.
-- **Canonical Family B `if` opens before the waits.** The standard pattern is `if [ "$monitor_rc" -eq 0 ]; then wait $PID; else wait $PID 2>/dev/null || true; fi`. The wait lines live inside the conditional, so the existing wait-identifier check finds them line-initial (per FINDING_2 fixtures must preserve that line-initial wait shape), and check (3) starts scanning from monitor_end_idx so the `if` is found before the wait.
-- **Suppression line position.** `line_has_lint_suppression` at the anchor line (the writer invocation) covers the whole helper call. Operators who only need to suppress the new monitor_rc checks have no separate token, but the helper-level suppression is acceptable because all checks belong to the same Family B wait/monitor contract.
-- **Existing test fixtures that intentionally test an `assert_case` (exit-1) failure mode.** When such a fixture anchors on a top-level Family B writer with a `wait` but currently lacks `monitor_rc`, the new lint will add three new error lines to the stderr. These tests already match a specific needle via `grep -Fq "$needle"` — extra stderr lines do not change the match outcome. Verify each `assert_case` fixture during the test update sweep; do not assume safety.
